\subsection{Fully-Connected Layers}
\label{sec: Dense-Layers}
The utterance-level speaker vector obtained from the pooling layer is fed into a set of four \gls{fc} layers (Figure \ref{FIG: systemDescription}). Each of the first two \gls{fc} layers is followed by a batch normalization \cite{ioffe2015batch} and \gls{relu} activations. A dense layer is adopted for the third \gls{fc} layer 
% without non-linearity and batch normalization 
and the last \gls{fc} corresponds to the speaker classification layer. Since \gls{ams} is used to train the network, the third layer is set up without activation and batch normalization as proposed in \cite{liu2019large}. Once the network is trained, we can extract a speaker embedding from one of the intermediate \gls{fc} layers. According to \cite{liu2019large}, we consider the second layer as the speaker embedding instead of the third one. The output of this \gls{fc} layer then corresponds to the speaker representation that will be used for the speaker verification task.

